            // If it reverts, rule trivially holds (no invariant on balance)
            // We only assert when it succeeds.
            // To make test meaningful, we need to ensure the withdraw does not revert in the reference
            // but might in the buggy version. Fuzzer will find amount that triggers difference.
            // The reference will succeed because no zero transfer to a reverting contract.
            // The buggy will try zero transfer to adversarialOwner, which reverts.
            // So reference will have success=true, balance 0; buggy will have success=false.
            // Assertion in reference: if (success) assert balance==0 (holds).
            // Buggy: success=false, so assertion skipped → test passes for buggy? No, need bug to FAIL assertion.
            // We need an assertion that fails for buggy. The above only asserts when success.
            // Better: force withdraw to succeed in reference but fail in buggy, then assert balance != 0?
            // We can't predict success in reference without more setup.
            // Let's rethink.
            // Rule: After any call to withdraw(), if it does not revert, contract balance must be 0.
